**HEURISTIC assessment:** I did not obtain a construction meeting (a)–(c). The most useful outcome is a **PROVED additional obstruction for ideal PTE over \(\mathbb Z\)**:
\[
P-Q=C\ne0,\quad \deg P=\deg Q=M,\quad P,Q\text{ monic and integer-split}
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\log |C|\ge \tfrac12M\log M-O(M).
\]
Repeated roots are allowed. This blocks the requested residual size when an ideal PTE class is compressed by one common integer offset. It does **not** cover independently recentering every residual modulo \(N\), or norm-only bounds in a general number field.

I read no existing repository files. Scratch programs, results, and a proof are linked below. Labels apply to the following paragraph, equations, or table until another label appears.

**PROVED — Counting conventions.** Write \(b=\log B\), and let \(D=[K:\mathbb Q]\). For number-field counts I use the house
\[
\|a\|_\infty=\max_\sigma|\sigma(a)|.
\]
For a fixed field,
\[
q_K(A):=\#\{a\in\mathcal O_K:\|a\|_\infty\le A\}=\Theta_K(A^D).
\]
There are exactly
\[
S_K(A,M)=\binom{q_K(A)+M-1}{M}
\]
monic degree-\(M\) polynomials split over this alphabet, counting multiplicities. Over \(\mathbb Z\), \(q=2\lfloor A\rfloor+1\).

**PROVED — Two accounting requirements.** If the actual integer pieces have absolute value at most \(B^{C_0}\), and \(|v|=o(N)\), nontriviality forces
\[
M\ge \frac{x-O(1)}{C_0b}.
\]
Also, a low-degree remainder need not have small coefficients, small evaluation, or small norm. In particular, taking a degree-\(M\) presentation \(F=P-R\) does not make the algebraic pieces \(\theta-a\) small-norm: their norms are \(F(a)\), potentially of size \(A^M\).

**PROVED — Scope of EX.** An exact polynomial identity is useful for constructing smoothness, but supplies no \(N\)-dependence by itself. A scalar evaluation condition \(F(m)\equiv0\pmod N\) is one congruence. Equality in a free rank-\(d\) algebra can impose \(d\) congruences. These should not be charged interchangeably.

---

**1. Dickson/Chebyshev compositions**

**PROVED — Explicit family and a correction.** With \(\tau+\tau^{-1}=t\),
\[
D_n(X)-D_n(t)
 =\prod_{j=0}^{n-1}
 \left(X-\tau\zeta_n^j-\tau^{-1}\zeta_n^{-j}\right).
\]
It splits in \(\mathbb Q(\zeta_n,\tau)\). The smaller field stated in the prompt is insufficient in general. For example,
\[
D_3(X)-D_3(3)=(X-3)(X^2+3X+6)
\]
has nonreal roots, whereas
\(\mathbb Q(\zeta_3+\zeta_3^{-1},\tau)=\mathbb Q(\sqrt5)\) is real.

**PROVED — Complete integer-\(t\) classification, including multiplicities.**

| Degree \(n\ge1\) | Integer \(t\) for which \(D_n(X)-D_n(t)\) splits over \(\mathbb Z\) |
|---|---|
| \(1,2\) | Every integer |
| \(3\) | \(-2,-1,1,2\) |
| \(4\) | \(-2,0,2\) |
| \(6\) | \(-2,-1,1,2\) |
| All other \(n\ge3\) | None |

**PROVED — Reason.** For \(|t|>2\) and \(n\ge3\), the displayed roots include a nonreal root. Thus only \(t\in\{-2,-1,0,1,2\}\) remain. Their Dickson values belong to \(\{-2,-1,0,1,2\}\). For value \(2\), a root is \(2\cos(2\pi/n)\); its rationality requires \(n\in\{1,2,3,4,6\}\). For value \(-2\), use \(2\cos(\pi/n)\), leaving \(n\le3\). For the three interior values, integer roots must lie in \(\{-1,0,1\}\), and all roots are simple, reducing the check to \(n\le3\). Direct substitution finishes the table.

**PROVED — Generalization of the \(D_6\) example.** For integers \(\lambda\ne0,h\),
\[
P_{\lambda,h}(X)
=\big((X-h)^2-4\lambda^2\big)
 \big((X-h)^2-\lambda^2\big)^2.
\]
There are \(\Theta(A^2)\) distinct such root multisets with roots bounded by \(A\). Any
\[
F_{\lambda,h,R}(X)=P_{\lambda,h}(X)-R(X)
\]
gives \(P_{\lambda,h}\equiv R\pmod F\). But varying \((\lambda,h)\) changes \(F\). If one degree-six \(F\) is fixed and remainders have degree below four, its leading coefficients fix \(h\) and \(\lambda^2\), so this two-parameter family disappears.

**PROVED — Fixed-field restriction.** Suppose \(n\ge3\) and \(D_n(X)-c\) splits in a fixed field \(K\). Choose a nonzero root \(r_0\) and label the roots as above. Then
\[
r_1+r_{-1}=(\zeta_n+\zeta_n^{-1})r_0,
\]
so \(\mathbb Q(\zeta_n+\zeta_n^{-1})\subseteq K\), and
\[
\varphi(n)/2\le D.
\]
Thus \(n\) is bounded when \(D\) is fixed. Composition cannot produce unbounded split degree in a fixed small field.

**PROVED using the KNOWN unit theorem — Density of shifts.** Put \(L=K(\zeta_n)\). From the roots one recovers \(\tau\in L\). Since \(\tau^2-r_0\tau+1=0\), integral roots make \(\tau\) an algebraic unit. Roots of house at most \(A\) give \(\|\tau\|_\infty\le A+1\). Consequently, for fixed \(K,n\ge3\),
\[
\#\{c:D_n(X)-c\text{ splits in }\mathcal O_K,\ 
             \text{roots of house }\le A\}
=O_{K,n}\big((\log A)^{r_L}\big),
\]
where \(r_L\) is the unit rank. Integer traces in a fixed quadratic Pell family give the familiar \(O(\log A)\) example. This follows from the logarithmic unit lattice. [Dirichlet’s unit theorem, Conrad](https://kconrad.math.uconn.edu/blurbs/gradnumthy/unittheorem.pdf)

**PROVED — Residual and \(N\)-dependence.** A common presentation
\[
F=D_n-H
\]
gives
\[
D_n(X)-c_i\equiv H(X)-c_i\pmod F.
\]
At a supplied root \(m\bmod N\), the residual is \(v_i=H(m)-c_i\). Here \(|c_i|\le2(A+1)^n\) in the integer realization. For arbitrary \(N\), obtaining a small \(H\) and small \(m\) with \(D_n(m)\equiv H(m)\pmod N\) is the missing alignment. Selecting \(F\) as an exact difference does not perform it.

**PROVED / UNVERIFIED — (a)–(c).** Fixed fields force bounded primitive degree, so (a) fails at subexponential \(B\). Growing cyclotomic fields abandon the fixed-small-field condition. Affine variants enumerate cheaply and can be genuinely different products, but no compatible, small, \(N\)-dependent common reduction was obtained. Galois relabelling of the complete root set gives the same polynomial.

---

**2. Prouhet–Tarry–Escott families**

**PROVED — Exact formulation.** Put \(k=M-t\). A PTE class is
\[
P_i(X)=T(X)+r_i(X),\qquad \deg r_i<t,
\]
where all \(P_i\) are monic and split, and \(T\) contains their common coefficients of degrees \(M,M-1,\ldots,t\). Newton identities identify this with equality of power sums through degree \(k\).

**PROVED — Residual height is the first problem.** Roots bounded by \(A\) imply
\[
|e_j|\le\binom Mj A^j.
\]
Although \(P_i-P_j\) has degree below \(t\), its coefficients can therefore have logarithmic size \(M\log A+O(M)\). Without additional cancellation,
\[
\log |(P_i-P_j)(m)|
\le M\log(A+|m|)+O(M).
\]
Small \(t\) alone gives no useful smallness.

**PROVED — Explicit structured families and their counts.**

- A fixed primitive PTE pair, with maximum entry \(H_0\), has \(\Theta_{H_0}(A^2)\) distinct integral affine images under \(a\mapsto \lambda a+h\). Each image remains a pair; different affine images generally have different common top parts.

- A concrete ideal quartic family is
  \[
  \{\pm a,\pm b\},\qquad a^2+b^2=R,
  \]
  with polynomial \(X^4-RX^2+a^2b^2\). A four-parameter identity is
  \[
  \begin{aligned}
  a&=ph-qk,& b&=pk+qh,\\
  c&=ph+qk,& d&=pk-qh,
  \end{aligned}
  \]
  giving \(a^2+b^2=c^2+d^2\). Parameters bounded by \(H\) give roots bounded by \(2H^2\), hence \(O(A^2)\) parameter choices. For one fixed \(R\le A^2\), the class size is \(r_2(R)/8\), apart from zero/equal-coordinate corrections. If \(R\) is a product of \(s\) distinct primes \(1\bmod4\), it has \(2^{s-1}\) members. The divisor bound gives class size \(A^{o(1)}\).

- Frolov lifting is explicit:
  \[
  A={}_kB
  \Longrightarrow
  A\cup(B+h)={}_{k+1}B\cup(A+h).
  \]
  After \(r\) lifts from size \(M_0\),
  \[
  M=M_0\,2^r,\qquad k=k_0+r.
  \]
  Entries are bounded by \(A_0+\sum|h_j|\); the \(r\) shifts give \(O(A^r)\) parameter choices. The defect is \(t=M-O(\log M)\), so compression is weak.

- Weighted Thue–Morse gives
  \[
  A_\pm=\left\{\sum_{j=1}^r\epsilon_jh_j:
          \epsilon_j\in\{0,1\},\
          (-1)^{\sum\epsilon_j}=\pm1\right\}.
  \]
  Each multiset has \(M=2^{r-1}\) entries and equal moments through \(k=r-1\), since
  \[
  \sum_\epsilon(-1)^{\sum\epsilon_j}
  e^{z\sum\epsilon_jh_j}
  =\prod_j(1-e^{zh_j})=O(z^r).
  \]
  Taking positive \(h_j\) with \(\sum h_j\le A\) gives \(O(A^r)\) parameter tuples. Again \(t=M-r+1\).

**KNOWN — Fixed-size ideal examples and chains.** Ideal examples at sizes through ten and at twelve are documented; for example,
\[
\{0,5,6,16,17,22\}={}_5\{1,2,10,12,20,21\},
\]
and the size-twelve pair
\[
\{\pm22,\pm61,\pm86,\pm127,\pm140,\pm151\},
\quad
\{\pm35,\pm47,\pm94,\pm121,\pm146,\pm148\}.
\]
I checked both exactly. These are fixed-degree examples, not a family with \(M\to\infty\). [Borwein–Lisoněk–Percival](https://www.daemonology.net/papers/pte.pdf)

**KNOWN — Long chains do exist.** Arbitrarily long ideal chains are known at some fixed small sizes; their existence should not be dismissed. General multigrade constructions also give \(j\)-member chains with size \(M=j^k\); Choudhry improves this to \(M=2j^{k-1}\). The latter still has defect \(M-k\), almost the whole degree. Numerous partitions are not automatically numerous distinct class members. [Choudhry](https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.11192)

**PROVED — General class-size bounds.** Let \(C\) be the number of distinct members in one class over an alphabet of size \(q\). Two distinct members cannot share a degree-\(t\) monic divisor. Therefore
\[
C\le \binom{q+t-1}{t},
\]
including repeated roots. For squarefree members, packing all their \(t\)-subsets gives the stronger
\[
C\binom Mt\le\binom qt.
\]
Moreover, if \(q\le k+1\), the moment equations of degrees \(0,\ldots,k\) determine every root multiplicity by a Vandermonde matrix. Thus a nontrivial class requires
\[
q\ge k+2=M-t+2.
\]
These statements also hold over a number field with \(q=q_K(A)\).

**HEURISTIC — Generic counting.** There are roughly \(A^M/M!\) integer root multisets and \(A^{k(k+1)/2}\) possible moment vectors, ignoring \(M\)-dependent factors. A naïve generic fiber size is consequently
\[
A^{M-k(k+1)/2}.
\]
When this is large, \(k\) is at most about \(\sqrt{2M}\), and the residual degree \(t=M-k\) remains large. For near-ideal classes the heuristic predicts exceptional fibers, rather than a broadly dense construction.

**KNOWN — What VMVT actually supplies.** For fixed \(M,k\), the Vinogradov mean value theorem gives
\[
J_{M,k}(A)\ll_{M,k,\epsilon}
 A^{M+\epsilon}
 +A^{2M-k(k+1)/2+\epsilon}.
\]
When \(k(k+1)/2\ge M\), this implies that the fraction of multisets lying in classes of size at least \(A^\delta\) tends to zero, for every fixed \(\delta>0\). It does not rule out exceptional large classes. Its constants must not be silently treated as uniform when \(M\) grows with \(N\). [Bourgain–Demeter–Guth, Theorem 1.1](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1512.01565)

**PROVED — The most constructive alignment.** If
\[
F=T-R_0,\qquad T(m)\equiv R_0(m)\pmod N,
\]
then
\[
u_i=P_i(m)=\prod_j(m-a_{ij}),\qquad
v_i=R_0(m)+r_i(m)
\]
is an explicit congruent family. This is the desired mechanism, conditional on finding the alignment and controlling the residuals.

For an ideal class, \(r_i=c_i=(-1)^M\prod_j a_{ij}\). Choosing \(R_0=0\) makes **both sides smooth by construction**:
\[
\prod_j(m-a_{ij})
 \equiv (-1)^M\prod_j a_{ij}\pmod N.
\]
This is worth retaining as a candidate even though the right side generally fails (b).

**HEURISTIC — Alignment cost.** If the available class parameters and \(m\)'s make \(T(m)\bmod N\) behave randomly, obtaining
\[
|T(m)\bmod N|\le e^y
\]
costs about \(e^{x-y}\) trials. A free rank-\(d\) coefficient alignment can instead have density approximately \((2H/N)^d\). A lattice or arithmetic parametrization could beat this trial model; no such efficient alignment was established here.

**PROVED — Ideal constants are smooth, but large.** If \(P_i-P_j=C\ne0\), evaluate at any root \(a\) of \(P_j\):
\[
C=P_i(a)=\prod_b(a-b).
\]
Hence every prime divisor of \(C\) is at most \(2A\). In particular, \(N\nmid C\) when all primes of \(N\) exceed \(2A\).

Here is the factorial lower bound. Each member of a nontrivial ideal pair has at least \(s=\lceil M/2\rceil\) distinct roots. Otherwise, for its support \(S\),
\[
W(X)=\prod_{a\in S}(X-a)^2
\]
has degree at most \(M-1\). Equal moments make its sums over the two root multisets equal. The first sum is zero and the second strictly positive, because an ideal pair has disjoint root supports.

At a root of the other polynomial, the product defining \(C\) therefore contains at least \(s\) distinct nonzero integer differences. Their absolute product is at least
\[
\lfloor s/2\rfloor!\,\lceil s/2\rceil!,
\]
so
\[
\boxed{\log|C|\ge \tfrac12M\log M-O(M).}
\]
For squarefree members the stronger bound is \(M\log M-O(M)\).

**PROVED — A near-ideal extension.** If \(\deg(P-Q)<t\), put \(k=M-t\), \(s=\lfloor k/2\rfloor+1\), and let \(H\) be the coefficient height of \(P-Q\). Then
\[
\boxed{
H\ge
\frac{\lfloor s/2\rfloor!\,\lceil s/2\rceil!}
     {t\,2^{t-1}A^{t-1}}.
}
\]
Remove the common polynomial divisor, apply the support argument above, and evaluate a suitable derivative of \(P-Q\) at a root. The derivative is a product of nonzero integer differences. A full proof is saved in [ptecons_pte_height_lemma.md](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ptecons_1/ptecons_pte_height_lemma.md).

**PROVED / UNVERIFIED — Distinctness and (a)–(c).** Different PTE multisets can give genuinely different exponent vectors; they are not automatically one relation. But affine images are different classes, and multiplying already-known relations adds no information. Fixed-size ideal families fail the required growing factor count. Unwrapped ideal compression fails (b) by the factorial bound. Near-ideal families with growing \(M\), small residual height, enough independent rows, and cheap \(N\)-alignment remain **UNVERIFIED**.

---

**3. Cyclotomic and Galois orbits**

**PROVED — Explicit families and density.** In \(K\supseteq\mathbb Q(\zeta_n)\),
\[
P_{a,h}(X)=\prod_{j=0}^{n-1}(X-h-a\zeta_n^j)
=(X-h)^n-a^n.
\]
Integer \(a,h\) bounded by \(A\) give \(\Theta(A^2)\) members, up to finite symmetries. Allowing \(a,h\in\mathcal O_K\) gives \(\Theta_K(A^{2D})\) for fixed \(n,K\).

A projective version, with matrix entries bounded by \(A\), is
\[
H_g(X)
=\prod_j\big((c\zeta_n^j+d)X-(a\zeta_n^j+b)\big)
=(dX-b)^n-(a-cX)^n.
\]
There are \(\Theta_n(A^4)\) primitive integer matrices modulo the finite set stabilizer, for fixed \(n\ge3\). These are small-coefficient linear factors; the associated roots are generally nonintegral. Thus this count must not be advertised as an integer-root count.

**PROVED — Why the projective orbit does not compress.** Reducing \(X^n\) to a low-degree expression does not eliminate the mixed binomial terms in \((dX-b)^n\) and \((a-cX)^n\). Their ordinary product-size bound at \(m\) is
\[
\log |H_g(m)|=O\!\left(n\log(A(|m|+1))\right).
\]
Demanding the finite-field-style compatibility for sufficiently many shifts enters the supplied F′ obstruction.

**PROVED — Field degree.** Full cyclotomic splitting requires \(\varphi(n)\le D\), so \(n\) is bounded in a fixed small field. The set stabilizer of \(\mu_n\) has size \(2n\); the large characteristic-zero projective orbit above exists because the matrices range over infinitely many rational transformations, and it lacks the finite-field reduction identity.

**PROVED — Special \(N\).** If a pair \((n,m)\) with
\[
N\mid\Phi_n(m)
\]
is supplied, \(F=\Phi_n\) and the evaluation map are available and checkable without factoring \(N\). This is a valid special-input presentation. However,
\[
\operatorname{Norm}(m-\zeta_n)=\Phi_n(m)
\]
already has logarithmic size at least \(x\), and increasing \(n\) increases the field degree. Small root house does not imply small factor norm. The usual useful optimization is the special-NFS route; no smaller exponent follows from the orbit alone. [Pomerance’s NFS discussion](https://math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/PDF/paper109.pdf)

**KNOWN — Jacobi sums give real arithmetic relations.** For a known prime \(\ell\equiv1\pmod n\), let \(\chi\) have order \(n\). Then
\[
J_{a,b}=\sum_{z\in\mathbb F_\ell}\chi(z)^a\chi(1-z)^b
\]
lies in \(\mathbb Z[\zeta_n]\), and, in the nondegenerate cases,
\[
J_{a,b}\overline{J_{a,b}}=\ell.
\]
Stickelberger gives explicit ideal factorizations. These can yield distinct ideal-relation vectors, rather than merely identical equations. [Bernard–Kučera, including Proposition 5.1](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2109.13329)

**PROVED / UNVERIFIED — Density and limitation.** For each \(\ell\), there are \(O(n^2)\) indexed Jacobi sums. House at most \(A\) restricts \(\ell\le A^2\), giving the elementary upper count \(O(n^2A^2)\). Direct character-sum evaluation costs \(O(\ell)\) per sum. Neither this count nor the ideal factorization supplies a reduction tied to arbitrary \(N\). Taking a finite field of unknown characteristic dividing \(N\) would require the missing information.

**PROVED — Galois distinctness.** A full root product is invariant under Galois relabelling. Integer norms of conjugates are identical. Individual ideal-factorization vectors can be different and even independent, but a fixed-degree Galois orbit has at most \(D\) members. It cannot supply an asymptotically large family by itself.

**PROVED — Norm inputs are not automatically special enough.** Every integer has a trivial quadratic norm presentation:
\[
N=\operatorname{Norm}_{\mathbb Q(\sqrt{m^2-N})/\mathbb Q}
       \big(m+\sqrt{m^2-N}\big).
\]
The field parameters or element heights contain the size of \(N\). Multiplying the element by units leaves its norm unchanged. This supplies representations of the same norm, not automatically new smooth congruences with small residuals.

